#srcfig(
  scorecard((
    ("Contrast", 4, 5, "Ink against surface, at body size."),
    ("Distinctiveness", 3, 5, "How far the accent sits from the defaults."),
    ("Range", 4, 5, "Enough steps of ink and rule to build a page."),
    ("Restraint", 2, 5, "How many colours carry meaning at once."),
  )),
  caption: [
    A scorecard, drawn so its bars and rules can be judged. The ratings are
    placeholders in a specimen, not an assessment of this pack.#assess
  ],
  source: [none — assessment, not evidence],
)

#verdict(
  [The pack is legible at body size and coherent across the components.],
  [Read the page, not the palette: a colour that survives a swatch strip can still
  fail behind nine-point text.#assess],
  tone: "caution",
)

== Quotation and claim

#claim(
  [Design tokens live in a brand pack and nowhere else — no hex code is ever
  written into a Typst or mermaid file.],
  attribution: "report-maker, repository instructions",
  source: [@components],
)

#srcquote(
  "Everything here is built from the brand tokens, so a document assembled from these pieces looks like it came from the same house as every other one.",
  attribution: "report-maker engine",
  source: [@components],
  locator: "components.typ, header comment",
)

Both blocks set the words in the display face behind an accent rule, and both
carry their provenance in the same faint micro type @components. They are the
only places a report reproduces someone else's words, so they are deliberately
hard to confuse with our own prose.#assess
